WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:04.540
Just before we get into Today’s video, I
want to give a random YouTube plug.

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:07.910
Sometimes I’m browsing around YouTube and I stumbled across a channel that I think it’s

00:00:07.910 --> 00:00:13.690
criminally unsubscribed and that channel I
want to tell you about today is DUMB MONEY.

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:21.410
‘We are dumb money, we quit our day jobs
to do this…’

00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:26.060
This is more business focused channel.

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:28.630
I’m not getting paid for saying this or
anything like that.

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:30.880
It’s just a channel I stumbled across and
I really liked.

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:34.290
They have a few thousands subscribers and
I’d like to send them some more.

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:37.989
So you can have their links on the description
below.

00:00:37.989 --> 00:00:47.530
So now, let’s get on to this.

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:52.540
Nobody likes dictators but… what happens
when the alternative is even worse?

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:54.280
This is the story of Libya.

00:00:54.280 --> 00:01:07.220
This is the story of former prime minister
Gaddafi’s rise and fall.

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:09.570
Libya has more oil reserves than America.

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:12.110
In fact, they have the biggest oil reserves
in Africa.

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:17.400
Then again, production-wise, they are far
below most of the famous oil countries, even

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:21.490
below Iran, despite Trump’s sanctions.

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:22.600
Why?

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:27.170
Well… ever heard of the Arab Spring?

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:32.200
After that period of uprisings and general
destabilization, Libya is the country that

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:33.660
ended up worst off.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:37.750
Basically, there’s a lesson to be learned
from Libya: no matter how badly you think

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:40.619
a country is doing… it can do even worse.

00:01:40.619 --> 00:01:44.549
Have you ever seen the movie ‘The dictator’,
by Shasha Baron Cohen?

00:01:44.549 --> 00:01:53.939
Well, this parody was pretty much inspired
by MUHAMMAR EL GADDAFI: one of the craziest

00:01:53.939 --> 00:01:58.180
dictators of all time, who ruled Libya for
decades.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:01.869
Gaddafi was well known for having his own
harem of about 30 women.

00:02:01.869 --> 00:02:09.780
This harem was known as his Amazonian Guard.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:13.919
From the early 1970s onward, they remained
close by his side at all times.

00:02:13.919 --> 00:02:16.529
Sometimes the women took bullets for Gaddafi.

00:02:16.529 --> 00:02:21.049
One woman died and two were injured when the
Libyan leader was attacked in 1998.

00:02:21.049 --> 00:02:25.079
Some of the conditions to become part of the
guard were to make an oath of virginity, dress

00:02:25.079 --> 00:02:28.749
in camouflage, and maintain perfect nail polish
and makeup.

00:02:28.749 --> 00:02:32.730
Of course, there are thousands of reports
of rape and abuse that came to light after

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:34.010
Gaddafi’s death.

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:37.989
Surprisingly, during his lifetime, Gaddafi
always congratulated himself as a protector

00:02:37.989 --> 00:02:41.220
of Women’s rights in the Arab World.

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:46.659
But not only that!

00:02:46.659 --> 00:02:51.439
The self-proclaimed Brother Leader had a massive
fear of heights and could not fly more than

00:02:51.439 --> 00:02:55.559
eight hours over water… he also had some
peculiar demands when he stayed in hotels:

00:02:55.559 --> 00:03:00.430
he always had to stay on the first floor and
he would not climb more than 35 steps.

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:05.680
So yes, if we make a video ranking the most
extravagant dictators of all time, Gaddafi

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:07.730
would definitely be in the top 10.

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:11.720
Diplomacy-wise, Gaddafi is one of those dictators
that went from being America’s worst enemy

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:16.959
to a friend of the US before becoming, yet
again, the man America loved to hate.

00:03:16.959 --> 00:03:22.449
And now Libya is a chaotic state, where even
foreign oil companies have to make deals with

00:03:22.449 --> 00:03:25.069
warlords in order to keep pumping oil.

00:03:25.069 --> 00:03:26.489
Don’t believe me?

00:03:26.489 --> 00:03:32.709
Just look at this news.

00:03:32.709 --> 00:03:37.249
Repsol oil fields in Libya go back to Business
after a deal with raiders

00:03:37.249 --> 00:03:41.549
In this case, a Spanish oil company, Repsol,
had an agreement with the Libyan National

00:03:41.549 --> 00:03:42.890
Oil corporation in 2008.

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:47.250
Back then, they just had to agree with the
dictatorship to do business.

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:51.069
Then, during the Libyan civil war, a different
warlord took over Repsol facilities.

00:03:51.069 --> 00:03:55.779
And, finally, after a lot of struggle, in
2018, the oil company had to make an agreement

00:03:55.779 --> 00:03:57.909
with some local tribe.

00:03:57.909 --> 00:03:58.909
What happened here?

00:03:58.909 --> 00:04:00.939
What’s going on in Libya today?

00:04:00.939 --> 00:04:03.619
Why would an oil company make a deal with
a war lord?

00:04:03.619 --> 00:04:07.019
Today, we’re going to answer all of these
questions but, before we do, let’s take

00:04:07.019 --> 00:04:13.239
a look back at the history.

00:04:13.239 --> 00:04:15.309
THE MAN YOU LOVE TO HATE

00:04:15.309 --> 00:04:17.639
Ever seen the movie Naked gun?

00:04:17.639 --> 00:04:21.810
It starts with a scene where Gaddafi appears
next to the other bad guys of the moment:

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:27.530
Idi Amin, Ayatollah Khomeini, Gorbachev, Yasser
Arafat, Fidel Castro… they’re all gathered

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:29.660
in a room, making a plan to defeat the US.

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:35.080
Yes, my dear viewers, there was a time when
Gaddafi was considered one of the worst evils

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:36.080
on the planet.

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:37.080
Why?

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:43.449
Let’s go back in time.

00:04:43.449 --> 00:04:47.729
Libya was an Italian colony for several decades
in the early 1900s, then it became a monarchy

00:04:47.729 --> 00:04:55.120
until in 1969, Gaddafi rose to power.

00:04:55.120 --> 00:05:02.169
He believed in so-called Arab Socialism, a
strange cocktail of centralized economy, secularism,

00:05:02.169 --> 00:05:03.500
and cult of personality.

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:08.530
He even developed his own political philosophy,
writing an extremely influential book, “The

00:05:08.530 --> 00:05:09.530
Green Book”.

00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:13.500
He made countless appearances at Arab and
international gatherings, standing out not

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:19.169
just with his outlandish clothing, but also
because of his blunt speeches and odd behaviour.

00:05:19.169 --> 00:05:22.980
Even some socialist parties in Europe were
sponsored by him.

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:31.010
Oil could pay for a lot of influence in the
rest of the world.

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:33.940
But unlike other countries like Egypt, he
didn’t nationalize oil.

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:37.449
He put harder conditions on foreign oil companies,
but that was all.

00:05:37.449 --> 00:05:41.580
He challenged oil executives by stating "people
who have lived without oil for 5,000 years

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:46.750
can live without it again for a few years
in order to attain their legitimate rights".

00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:47.819
That was a bold move.

00:05:47.819 --> 00:05:52.130
A move that helped him secure a majority share
of oil company revenues while, at the same

00:05:52.130 --> 00:05:55.520
time, maintaining an effective oil industry
in the country.

00:05:55.520 --> 00:06:03.960
Despite the socialism and the dictatorship,
Libya was still open for business.

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:08.870
Repsol says Libyan oil nationalisation unlikely
And, let’s be honest here… the formula

00:06:08.870 --> 00:06:09.870
worked.

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:14.069
Many European companies like ENI, TOTAL and
Repsol kept doing business with Libya.

00:06:14.069 --> 00:06:16.600
Money came in, oil came out.

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:18.490
And the country’s citizens benefited.

00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:24.569
For years, Libya had the highest Human Development
Index of any African country, at certain points,

00:06:24.569 --> 00:06:25.990
surpassing South Africa.

00:06:25.990 --> 00:06:29.280
This increase was accompanied by a very controversial
foreign policy.

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:34.449
During the 1980s and 1990s, Gaddafi aligned
himself with the Eastern Bloc, Fidel Castro’s

00:06:34.449 --> 00:06:39.440
Cuba and openly supported movements like the
Irish Republican Army and the Palestinian

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:40.580
Liberation Front.

00:06:40.580 --> 00:06:47.040
But none of this would have been a big deal
if it wasn’t for the terrorism.

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:53.880
Libya’s government was known to participate
or aide in terrorist acts.

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:58.550
Many Marxist-inspired guerrilla groups or
even terrorist groups were funded by Gaddafi.

00:06:58.550 --> 00:07:02.419
But the defining moment was the bombing of
a nightclub in Berlin where American soldiers

00:07:02.419 --> 00:07:03.509
used to frequent.

00:07:03.509 --> 00:07:05.690
Many Libyan spies were involved in this.

00:07:05.690 --> 00:07:07.289
And America didn’t wait to answer.

00:07:07.289 --> 00:07:12.550
‘At 7 o clock this evening, eastern time,
air and naval forces of the United States

00:07:12.550 --> 00:07:18.710
launched a series of strikes against the headquarters,
terrorist facilities and military assets that

00:07:18.710 --> 00:07:21.729
support Muhammar Gaddafi’s subversive activities’

00:07:21.729 --> 00:07:26.139
The harsh reaction was supposed to kill the
“mad dog of the Middle East”, as Reagan

00:07:26.139 --> 00:07:30.449
branded Gaddafi, but the Colonel came out
of the attack unscathed.

00:07:30.449 --> 00:07:33.560
America put Libya on the terrorist-friendly
countries list and American companies were

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:35.740
restricted from exploiting oil there.

00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:49.259
Nonetheless, those European companies like
Total, Repsol, and ENI kept doing their thing.

00:07:49.259 --> 00:07:57.240
But let’s press fast forward on our story.

00:07:57.240 --> 00:07:58.770
In 2001, 9/11 happened.

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:05.770
And this was a turning point for Gaddafi’s
foreign policy.

00:08:05.770 --> 00:08:10.389
Terrorist-loving as he was, Gaddafi was inspired
by socialism, not radical Islamism.

00:08:10.389 --> 00:08:15.220
So Osama Bin Laden was a common enemy, both
for America and for many socialist leaders.

00:08:15.220 --> 00:08:20.189
This is how, Gaddafi started collaborating
with America by signing a nuclear disarmament

00:08:20.189 --> 00:08:23.610
agreement, in which the leader agreed to eliminate
his country’s weapons of mass destruction.

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:27.039
The US declared victory, and everything was
good again.

00:08:27.039 --> 00:08:30.610
In 2006 they even took Libya off the list
of terrorist countries.

00:08:30.610 --> 00:08:33.399
Gaddafi was a son of a bitch… but now he
was OUR son of a bitch.

00:08:33.399 --> 00:08:35.289
(((Please, pronounce the word “son of a
bitch” like that.

00:08:35.289 --> 00:08:36.700
I will add some censorship on post production)))

00:08:36.700 --> 00:08:44.070
Nonetheless, again, things changed in 2011…

00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:45.529
THE ARAB SPRING

00:08:45.529 --> 00:08:50.500
The Arab Spring began in December 2010 when
Mohammed Bouazizi, a Tunisian street vendor,

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:53.990
set himself on fire in protest against the
police.

00:08:53.990 --> 00:08:58.190
He could have never imagined how his sacrifice
would spark a revolution.

00:08:58.190 --> 00:09:01.870
All of a sudden, the Tunisian people started
protesting against their government, which

00:09:01.870 --> 00:09:04.690
was another Arab socialist dictatorship.

00:09:04.690 --> 00:09:07.790
And this inspired protesters in neighboring
countries.

00:09:07.790 --> 00:09:12.240
Suddenly, most of the Northern African countries
were overthrowing their dictators.

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:13.670
Libya was not the exception.

00:09:13.670 --> 00:09:19.810
In early 2011, a civil war broke out.

00:09:19.810 --> 00:09:27.640
When the first winds of rebellion started
blowing towards Libya, Gaddafi even tried

00:09:27.640 --> 00:09:32.269
to protest with the people, trying to keep
up with this “brother leader of the revolution”

00:09:32.269 --> 00:09:34.899
image that he was so desperate to keep.

00:09:34.899 --> 00:09:38.190
But, turns out, people could see through his
efforts.

00:09:38.190 --> 00:09:41.209
The scent of freedom was inebriating.

00:09:41.209 --> 00:09:45.339
Many young Libyans were fueled by the recent
toppling of Egypt’s Mubarak and Tunisia’s

00:09:45.339 --> 00:09:46.339
Ben Ali.

00:09:46.339 --> 00:09:55.190
That’s when Gaddafi changed his tune and
reverted back to being ruthless.

00:09:55.190 --> 00:09:58.040
Gaddafi tells the media: 'All my people love
me.

00:09:58.040 --> 00:10:02.370
They would die to protect me' Libyan leader
laughs off international pressure to step

00:10:02.370 --> 00:10:07.080
down while speaking to news organizations
Within days, opposition supporters were controlling

00:10:07.080 --> 00:10:10.540
Benghazi and the government was seeing massive
rates of defection.

00:10:10.540 --> 00:10:14.130
The rebels tried organizing even further,
but they were being hit pretty hard by the

00:10:14.130 --> 00:10:15.399
Colonel’s forces.

00:10:15.399 --> 00:10:20.199
As you can imagine, Gaddafi had an army much
bigger than those 30 women from his Amazon

00:10:20.199 --> 00:10:21.199
guard.

00:10:21.199 --> 00:10:23.730
Libyan oil helped to pay for a lot of modern
weaponry.

00:10:23.730 --> 00:10:26.980
And this was pretty helpful in repressing
protesters.

00:10:26.980 --> 00:10:35.600
But, as so many people lost their lives, the
image of Gaddafi outside of Libya couldn’t

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:36.940
get worse.

00:10:36.940 --> 00:10:40.630
Once again, he went from being OUR bad guy
to the man everyone loved to hate.

00:10:40.630 --> 00:10:45.509
And this is why a coalition led by NATO’s
forces invaded Libya on the 21st of March

00:10:45.509 --> 00:10:54.910
2011 in support of the rebels.

00:10:54.910 --> 00:10:57.320
But it was months before NATO could turn the
tables.

00:10:57.320 --> 00:11:01.850
As the fighting intensified, NATO carried
out a bombing campaign in support of anti-Gaddafi

00:11:01.850 --> 00:11:02.850
forces.

00:11:02.850 --> 00:11:06.959
The bombing forced Gaddafi to flee from Tripoli
to his hometown of Sirte, where opposition

00:11:06.959 --> 00:11:12.510
fighters would eventually capture him.

00:11:12.510 --> 00:11:18.120
On October 20, 2011, a video released by the
fighters shows Gaddafi begging for his life.

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:22.089
In another clip, the former strongman is seen
dead, with an apparent gunshot wound to the

00:11:22.089 --> 00:11:23.089
head.

00:11:23.089 --> 00:11:27.980
Muammar Gaddafi was caught and shot dead by
the militias of the Libyan National Transitional

00:11:27.980 --> 00:11:31.329
Council, with the active support of French
Intelligence.

00:11:31.329 --> 00:11:35.230
Many of you would be tempted to say SIC SEMPER
TYRANNIS, right?

00:11:35.230 --> 00:11:39.449
Well… wait a minute.

00:11:39.449 --> 00:11:41.290
FROM BAD TO EVEN WORSE

00:11:41.290 --> 00:11:45.300
After the corpse of the former dictator was
dragged through the streets and put on display,

00:11:45.300 --> 00:11:46.990
the rebels turned on each other.

00:11:46.990 --> 00:11:51.779
The country was split, and crime and human
trafficking ran rampant.

00:11:51.779 --> 00:11:57.120
And if there’s one rule in politics is this:
THERE CANNOT BE A POWER VACUUM.

00:11:57.120 --> 00:12:01.270
Gaddafi was gone but… who or what was the
alternative?

00:12:01.270 --> 00:12:05.259
Even though some western countries had been
doing good business with Gaddafi, they never

00:12:05.259 --> 00:12:11.230
fully trusted him, of course.

00:12:11.230 --> 00:12:15.620
They saw the civil war as an opportunity to
remove him from power and intervene militarily

00:12:15.620 --> 00:12:20.320
in order to cut off the Arab revolution that
had flared up in Tunisia and Egypt.

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:24.670
It also provided powers such as France and
Britain a chance to reassert themselves on

00:12:24.670 --> 00:12:25.780
the world stage.

00:12:25.780 --> 00:12:29.790
After Gaddafi’s death, Libya was torn up
and divided, without a national government

00:12:29.790 --> 00:12:30.790
to claim control.

00:12:30.790 --> 00:12:35.089
And what that meant at the time was that local
militias now had the power of life and death

00:12:35.089 --> 00:12:36.089
over the population.

00:12:36.089 --> 00:12:40.690
The economy was also messed up, as one might
expect after a revolution.

00:12:40.690 --> 00:12:46.430
The GDP collapsed in 2014 and in 2015 the
production of crude oil fell to the lowest

00:12:46.430 --> 00:12:49.830
level on record since the 1970s.

00:12:49.830 --> 00:12:58.839
Ex-NATO boss: Libya still a 'model intervention'
You see, unlike the Iraq or Afghanistan war,

00:12:58.839 --> 00:13:00.449
the Libyan one was a different animal.

00:13:00.449 --> 00:13:04.269
In this case, it was not led by America but,
instead, by France.

00:13:04.269 --> 00:13:10.130
And the French military style couldn’t be
more different.

00:13:10.130 --> 00:13:15.029
Usually, the Americans invade a country, kill
the dictator, and then stay there for years.

00:13:15.029 --> 00:13:20.880
We’ve seen this in Japan and Germany after
World War II but also in Iraq and Afghanistan.

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:26.060
The military tries to make sure they can form
a post war government and fight the last groups

00:13:26.060 --> 00:13:27.370
that supported the former one.

00:13:27.370 --> 00:13:28.959
The French are pretty much the opposite.

00:13:28.959 --> 00:13:31.360
They strike, kill the bad guy, and leave.

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:33.360
This is what happened in Mali, for example.

00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:35.310
And this is what happened in Libya.

00:13:35.310 --> 00:13:38.390
So, of course, effectiveness wise, the Libyan
war was a complete success.

00:13:38.390 --> 00:13:42.709
In fact, if you look at this chart, you can
see that it was one of the cheapest wars in

00:13:42.709 --> 00:13:43.709
modern history.

00:13:43.709 --> 00:13:45.690
It took a little over a month.

00:13:45.690 --> 00:13:55.779
Compared with Iraq or Afghanistan, the Libyan
war couldn’t be more cost effective.

00:13:55.779 --> 00:13:57.360
So what’s the problem?

00:13:57.360 --> 00:14:00.029
Well, the aftermath.

00:14:00.029 --> 00:14:06.450
After the fall of Gaddafi, three main players
emerged in the country: the government of

00:14:06.450 --> 00:14:10.470
Tripoli, supported by Turkey and Qatar, the
government of Tobruk, supported mainly by

00:14:10.470 --> 00:14:16.690
Egypt and the Arab Emirates and, last but
not least, the DAESH forces.

00:14:16.690 --> 00:14:22.110
War raged once more and DAESH took advantage
of the chaos, expanding rapidly.

00:14:22.110 --> 00:14:25.779
The elections in 2014 were marred by violence
and low turnout.

00:14:25.779 --> 00:14:31.350
Eventually, a compromise was found, and a
Government of National Agreement was formed,

00:14:31.350 --> 00:14:32.610
sponsored by the UN.

00:14:32.610 --> 00:14:37.930
A group that monitors violence using media
reports estimated that more than 4,600 people

00:14:37.930 --> 00:14:44.240
have died in the fighting since the beginning
of 2014 and the U.N. estimates 2.44 million

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:47.920
people — about a third of Libya’s population
— have been affected by the fighting, which

00:14:47.920 --> 00:14:53.490
has led to shortages of food, water, electricity
and medical supplies and reduced access to

00:14:53.490 --> 00:15:01.580
health care and public services
Forgotten war: a crisis deepens in Libya but

00:15:01.580 --> 00:15:02.700
where are the cameras?

00:15:02.700 --> 00:15:06.370
As you might have noticed, Libya isn’t doing
much better today...

00:15:06.370 --> 00:15:10.870
The government installed in 2016 only has
a partial hold on the country.

00:15:10.870 --> 00:15:15.139
Given this instability, there are no facilities
and desert patrols to tackle trafficking networks.

00:15:15.139 --> 00:15:19.310
There are also no funds for Libya's coastguard
to monitor sea crossings

00:15:19.310 --> 00:15:24.079
And this is why, in this dangerous power vacuum,
oil companies are even starting to make deals

00:15:24.079 --> 00:15:27.010
with local tribes in order to keep their businesses
going.

00:15:27.010 --> 00:15:36.100
The Repsol camp in Libya resumes activity
after the agreement with the assailants

00:15:36.100 --> 00:15:40.600
Despite all the turmoil, Repsol is still going
strong in Libya and is currently preparing

00:15:40.600 --> 00:15:42.790
to increase production even more.

00:15:42.790 --> 00:15:45.100
Money really does talk.

00:15:45.100 --> 00:15:50.029
Now the question is… if even big oil companies
are making deals with rebels, does a unified

00:15:50.029 --> 00:15:52.709
Libyan government really have a chance?

00:15:52.709 --> 00:15:56.319
Will we ever see a democratic and stable government
in this country?

00:15:56.319 --> 00:15:59.540
Please, leave your answers in the comment
section below.

00:15:59.540 --> 00:16:03.529
And of course, don’t forget to visit our
friends from RECONSIDER MEDIA.Com, the podcast

00:16:03.529 --> 00:16:05.699
